Kubernetes is like a theater’s stage crew. The audience sees the actors (your app). They never see the crew replacing a burnt-out spotlight mid-scene (healing a crashed pod). 🎭

I wrote about how K8s works, its core components, and why its invisibility is the whole point. 👇
[Link to post]


The best infrastructure is the kind you forget exists.

In my latest post, I break down Kubernetes using the analogy of a theater stage crew. We often talk about K8s in terms of Pods, Deployments, and Control Planes (and I cover those!), but the real magic of Kubernetes is its shift to declarative infrastructure.

You don't script the recovery; you declare the state, and the system quietly closes the gap.

If you've ever found K8s intimidating, this one is for you. I break down:
🛠️ What Pods & Services actually do
🧠 How the Control Plane acts as the "brain"
⚠️ Why this invisibility becomes a liability when things break

